NAME
    grep - search input lines for patterns

SYNOPSIS
    grep [-EFGivnbcqsowxhHlL] [-e PATTERN] [-m NUM]
         [PATTERN] [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
    Selects lines matching any pattern. The default syntax is a POSIX-style
    basic regular expression (BRE); -E selects extended expressions (ERE),
    and -F selects literal strings. With no FILE, reads standard input; at an
    interactive prompt that reads typed lines until Ctrl-D, see man sh. A FILE
    of - also selects standard input. Multiple files receive filename prefixes
    by default.

    The bounded byte-oriented engine supports grouping, alternation,
    concatenation, ., ^, $, *, +, ?, intervals such as {2,4}, bracket ranges,
    negated brackets, and the ASCII POSIX classes alnum, alpha, blank, cntrl,
    digit, graph, lower, print, punct, space, upper, and xdigit. BRE grouping,
    alternation, +, ?, and intervals use backslashes. Backreferences,
    locale-specific collation, recursion, context output, and pattern files are
    not implemented. Programs are limited to 512 instructions, 16 patterns,
    nesting depth 32, and repetition bounds up to 64.

OPTIONS
    -E  Interpret patterns as extended regular expressions.
    -F  Interpret patterns as fixed strings.
    -G  Interpret patterns as basic regular expressions; this is the default.
    -e PATTERN  Add a pattern. May be repeated; attached -ePATTERN is accepted.
    -i  Compare ASCII letters without regard to case.
    -v  Select nonmatching lines.
    -n  Prefix selected output with its one-based line number.
    -b  Prefix selected output with its zero-based byte offset.
    -c  Print only the selected-line count for each input.
    -q  Write no output and stop when the result is known.
    -s  Suppress filesystem error messages.
    -o  Print each nonempty matching portion on a separate line.
    -w  Require matches to be bounded by non-word bytes.
    -x  Require a match of the complete line.
    -h  Suppress filename prefixes.
    -H  Always print filename prefixes.
    -l  Print only names of inputs containing a selected line.
    -L  Print only names of inputs containing no selected line.
    -m NUM  Stop after NUM selected lines per input.

    Options may be combined when they take no argument. -- ends option parsing.
    Status is zero when the requested selection succeeds, one when it does not,
    and another nonzero status for invalid patterns or I/O errors.
